Let $U$ be a nonempty subset of $\Rn$ and let $T:U \to\Rn$. Then $T$  is \emph{firmly nonexpansive}
 if %$(\forall x,y\in U)$ $\norm{Tx-Ty}^2+\norm{\left(\Id-T\right)x-\left(\Id-T %\right)y}^2\leq\norm{x-y}^2$;
$(\forall x,y\in U)\ \norm{Tx-Ty}^2\leq \ip{x-y}{Tx-Ty}$;
$T$ is \emph{$\lambda$-cocoercive} with $\lambda>0$ if $\lambda T$ is firmly nonexpansive;
$T$ is \emph{nonexpansive} if $(\forall x,y\in U)\ \norm{Tx-Ty}\leq\norm{x-y}$; and $T$ is \emph{averaged} if
there exist nonexpansive $N:U\to\Rn$ and $0\leq \lambda<1$ such that $T:=(1-\lambda)\Id+\lambda N$.
We use $\Fix T$ for the set of fixed point of $T$.
See \cite[Chapter 4]{BC} for abundant results and applications of nonexpansive operators.

